 "paper\_count": 17,
 "executive\_summary": "Across 17 distinct studies, the literature supports decomposing reliability into multiple independently measurable dimensions rather than treating factuality, attribution, completeness, temporal validity, confidence, and abstention as one score. Citation presence or topical relevance does not establish factual support, and source support does not establish source truth or currency. Evidence localization can improve verification and human review, while retrieval remains a major source of error and additional context can sometimes degrade synthesis. Temporal ordering and benchmark aging introduce failure modes invisible to static claim checking. Raw model confidence is often miscalibrated, particularly under domain shift, and contradictory evidence produces substantial over-answering. Automatic judges can assist evaluation but require human validation because aggregate agreement can conceal instance-level errors, directional bias, abstention effects, and metric gaming. Direct evidence for private workplace communication remains absent.",
 "themes": [
  "Reliability is multidimensional: answer correctness, evidence support, citation precision, citation completeness, source accessibility, temporal validity, confidence calibration, and abstention measure different failure modes.",
  "Claim-level or sentence-level evidence localization improves traceability and can assist human verification, but localized support does not establish the truth or freshness of the underlying source.",
  "Retrieval quality materially affects verification, yet evidence presence alone does not guarantee correct evidence use, and increasing retrieved context can introduce additional synthesis failures.",
  "Temporal reliability requires reasoning about ordering, observation time, and changing facts rather than checking isolated propositions against static evidence.",
  "Accuracy and model confidence are distinct: high task accuracy can coexist with overconfidence, domain-transfer calibration error, and unsafe answering under insufficient or contradictory evidence.",
  "Automatic evaluators and LLM judges require explicit measurement protocols, human validation, directional error analysis, and careful treatment of abstentions.",
