  "paper\_id": "2004.14974",
  "title": "Fact or Fiction: Verifying Scientific Claims",
  "access": "full\_text",
  "research\_question": "How can a system retrieve scientific evidence, identify the exact rationale sentences supporting a decision, and determine whether an individual scientific source supports or refutes a claim?",
  "methodology": "The authors introduce SciFact, containing 1,409 expert-written scientific claims evaluated against a corpus of 5,183 scientific abstracts, with supporting or refuting abstracts annotated at sentence-level rationale granularity. They develop VeriSci, a three-stage pipeline consisting of TF-IDF abstract retrieval, a BERT-based rationale selector, and a BERT-based Supports/Refutes/NoInfo classifier. Models are evaluated in both oracle-evidence and open-retrieval settings using sentence- and abstract-level precision, recall, and F1, with 10,000 bootstrap resamples for uncertainty analysis. A separate case study applies the system to COVID-19 claims against CORD-19.",
